  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Winter Weather Advisories continue for central Idaho, the\n   Bitterroot/Sapphire Mountains, and Southwest Montana for heavy\n   mountain snow and slick valley travel.\n\n-  Falling snow levels overnight, with increasing travel impacts\n   across mountain passes and southwest Montana.\n\nA slow moving Pacific trough continues to bring widespread\nprecipitation across the Northern Rockies this afternoon.\nObservations suggest snow levels between 3,500-4,000 feet, with\narea web cameras showing winter driving conditions at times \nacross Lolo and Lost Trail Passes and Montana Highway 1 near \nGeorgetown Lake. Looking ahead to this evening, a mid-level closed\nlow will move along and east of the Continental Divide into \ncentral Montana, with northwest flow leading to cold air \nadvection. This will bring snow levels down overnight, with many \nvalley areas in western Montana switching over to snow. \nTemperatures will drop into the low-30s, but recent warmth has \nkept ground temperatures from freezing. Therefore, winter weather\nadvisories have not been issued for many western Montana valley\nareas, as snow will be wet and slushy rather than frozen and icy \ngiven the temperature profile. Winter weather advisories remain \nin effect tonight into early tomorrow morning across higher \nelevations of north-central Idaho, the Bitterroot/Sapphire \nMountains, and Southwest Montana, where confidence is higher for \ncolder temperatures and widespread snow to impact travel. \n\nLooking ahead to the rest of the week, the western US will be\nunder the influence of an active Pacific storm cycle. A series of\nshortwave troughs will move through, with the majority of moisture\nand dynamics moving into California and the southwest US. The\nNorthern Rockies will be on the far northern periphery of this\nstorm track. \n\nThe next storm disturbance arrives Thursday morning as a Pacific\ntrough moves into the Great Basin. Southwesterly flow aloft will\nbring shower activity into the area, with snow levels near 2,000\nfeet along and north of I-90, climbing near 3,000-3,500 feet south\nof I-90. Overall, this system doesn't have much moisture to work \nwith, but localized accumulations up to 1 inch may bring travel \nimpacts, particularly over the higher terrain. This system will be\nmonitored closely for any changes in the next day or two. \n\nValley snow chances are also being monitored Friday into Saturday\nalong and north of the I-90 corridor. Model guidance suggests a \nshortwave trough will drop southward from Canada into north-\ncentral Montana. This will place the area under a northwest flow,\nwith probabilities for snow highest (70-90%) across the Flathead\nValley, US-93 northward to Eureka, and the Glacier Park region.\nSnow chances decrease to 30-40% towards the I-90 corridor.\nFurthermore, colder temperatures in the forecast in the coming\ndays may allow for snow to readily accumulate by Friday.",
